Ladybird’s WebContent now sends the UI process only the accessibility
nodes that changed since the last tree it sent, numbered so the UI
process can tell when it has to ask for the whole tree again. The
other engines send incremental updates too. Blink’s AXTreeSerializer::
SerializeChanges() serializes only the dirty nodes into an AXTreeUpdate,
and sets its node_id_to_clear to have the browser drop a subtree
it has to replace. Gecko’s content process sends ShowEvent and
HideEvent messages for structural changes, and DocAccessibleChild
sends cache updates marked CacheUpdateType::Update after the
CacheUpdateType::Initial one. WebKit keeps its accessibility tree in the
web process, so it has no tree to send.
